# Line endings.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on at least one machine here, which means the working
# tree it checks out gets CRLF while the repository stores LF. That is fine
# until a tool writes a file back with the endings it found, and then a commit
# carries a whole-file ending change nobody made. Declaring the rule in the
# repo takes the decision away from each clone's local config.
#
# LF everywhere: in the repository and in the working tree, on every platform.

* text=auto

# Source and content — LF in the working tree too, not just in the repository.
*.ts        text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.cjs       text eol=lf
*.astro     text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.svg       text eol=lf
*.xml       text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.patch     text eol=lf
.gitignore    text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Binaries — never inspected, never converted.
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.png   binary
*.webp  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
